## 2. Semantic Packet Design (Short Keys)

To minimize the size of the transmitted packet (the primary objective of semantic communication), we map semantic fields to short 1-2 character JSON keys:

| Full Field | Short Key | Type | Description / Example |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **intent** | `i` | String | Extracted verb/action (e.g., `send`, `cancel`, `alert`) |
| **person** | `p` | String | Target individual or role (e.g., `officer`, `john`) |
| **object** | `o` | String | Target object of action (e.g., `package`, `water`, `truck`) |
| **quantity**| `q` | Integer | Exact count (e.g., `5`, `100`) |
| **location**| `l` | String | Place name (e.g., `Chennai`, `Sector-4`) |
| **date** | `d` | String | ISO Date format `YYYY-MM-DD` (e.g., `2026-08-26`) |
| **time** | `t` | String | 24h Time format `HH:MM` (e.g., `15:00`) |
| **negation**| `n` | Boolean | True if negation is detected (e.g., `do not`, `cancel`, `never`) |
| **urgency** | `u` | Boolean | True if urgent (e.g., `urgent`, `asap`, `immediately`) |
| **warning** | `w` | Boolean | True if threat/warning (e.g., `warning`, `danger`, `alert`) |

